# Supagloo local-dev env scheme (Docker Compose stack).
#
# These are the DEV defaults matching docker-compose.yml. The root smoke-test
# harness reads them with these exact fallbacks, so copying this file is
# optional for local `docker compose up`. Later tasks (api/dbos, tasks 8/13/15)
# consume the same vars inside the containers.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local   # optional; overrides the built-in fallbacks

# --- Postgres (single postgres:17 container, two logical databases) ---
POSTGRES_USER=supagloo
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=supagloo
# App schema (Prisma-managed) and DBOS system db. From the HOST use localhost;
# from inside the Compose network use host `postgres`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://supagloo:supagloo@localhost:5432/supagloo
DBOS_DATABASE_URL=postgres://supagloo:supagloo@localhost:5432/supagloo_dbos

# --- Application secrets key (Task #12, design-delta §2.10) ---
# The single AES-256-GCM key the api/dbos containers use to encrypt/decrypt
# per-user provider secrets at rest (the OpenRouter API key, the Gloo client
# secret) — NOT itself per-user data; one key per deployment, shared by both
# services (a mismatch between them means decryption fails with AUTH_FAILED).
# 64-char hex (32 bytes). The value below is the same well-known dev-only
# placeholder used in supagloo-database-lib/.env.example — never use it in prod.
#
# DISTINCT PER ENVIRONMENT (plan row 43 / D43.1). The rule has two halves and they pull
# in opposite directions, so state both:
#
#   * IDENTICAL WITHIN an environment. api and dbos must carry the SAME value or
#     `decryptSecret` returns AUTH_FAILED for anything the other service wrote.
#     design-delta §11.7 records that exact incident. Machine-enforced by root's
#     `tests/unit/compose-config.test.ts` PART V invariant 5.
#   * DISTINCT ACROSS environments. Every non-dev deployment supplies its OWN key:
#
#         openssl rand -hex 32
#
#     Neither service can check that from inside its own process (a container cannot see
#     another deployment's key), so the enforceable parts are: api and dbos both REJECT an
#     all-zeros key outright, and PART V invariant 7 confines the well-known dev value
#     below to this file and `docker-compose.yml` and requires every occurrence to be
#     labelled dev-only. Rotating it means rotating BOTH services at once — every
#     already-encrypted provider credential in the database becomes undecryptable.
#
# NEVER give this variable to the `nextjs` service. It has no database and no S3 access
# and never calls encryptSecret/decryptSecret (design-delta §5.1), and its boot validator
# now REFUSES TO START if the variable is present at all.
SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef

# --- GitHub App (Task #11/#17, design-delta §2.3/§7) ---
# Required (fail-fast at boot) for BOTH the api and dbos containers — unlike the
# vars above, these have NO safe dev-only default (they're tied to a real
# registered GitHub App), so copying this file to a root .env is NOT optional
# once you need `docker compose up` to actually boot api/dbos.
#
# One pair per app registration, shared by api + dbos. api signs ~10-min App
# JWTs (GITHUB_APP_ID issuer + GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY) to verify installations
# and mint installation tokens, and builds the hosted install-picker URL
# (.../apps/{GITHUB_APP_SLUG}/installations/new); dbos's git-ops workflows sign
# their own App JWTs the same way to mint installation tokens directly (no HTTP
# hop through the api). The private key is the app's PKCS#1/PKCS#8 PEM as a
# SINGLE LINE with escaped \n (normalized to real newlines before signing) —
# e.g. `-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIE...\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n`.
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
GITHUB_APP_SLUG=

# Task #26 create-new-repo JIT hop (design-delta §2.3/§6b) — api-only (dbos has
# no user context). The App's OAuth-style user-to-server client id/secret: it
# exchanges a short-lived user authorization code for a per-request user access
# token, used once to create a repo under the user's account, then discarded.
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- GitHub e2e harness PAT (real-GitHub e2e — TEST-ONLY, never product code) ---
# REQUIRED by the e2e suites that drive git-ops against the REAL GitHub API: a
# missing value must FAIL the e2e global-setup fast with an actionable message.
# Unused (and unread) by the api/dbos/nextjs services themselves — no product
# code path touches this var.
#
# Why a PAT is needed at all, when the GitHub App creds above already exist:
# the App's INSTALLATION token is scoped to contents:write + pull_requests:write
# + metadata:read, which covers clone/commit/push/branch/tag/PR/merge but NOT
# repository CREATION or ARCHIVAL (that needs `administration`, which the
# install does not grant — and `POST /user/repos` is user-scoped regardless, so
# an installation token can never create a repo in a personal account).
# The product solves this with an interactive user-authorization hop, which
# cannot be completed headlessly; the e2e harness uses this PAT instead, for
# fixture-repo lifecycle ONLY.
#
# Scopes: `repo`, and NOTHING else. `repo` alone covers the entire fixture-repo
# lifecycle this harness performs — create, push, and archive.
#
# Do NOT grant `delete_repo`. There is no hard-delete escape hatch anywhere in
# this project and there must never be one: the cleanup script ARCHIVES
# (`PATCH {"archived": true}`) and issues no DELETE on any path, because
# archiving is reversible and deletion is not. That is enforced code, not a
# convention — it is safety property 1 in `scripts/cleanup-e2e-repos.mjs`, and
# `tests/unit/cleanup-e2e-repos.test.ts` asserts that no code path ever issues a
# DELETE. A token carrying `delete_repo` therefore buys this project nothing and
# only widens the blast radius in an account that also holds your real repos.
# Mint at: https://github.com/settings/tokens/new
#
# Used by: e2e fixture setup (creates `supagloo-e2e-delete-me-*` repos) and the
# cleanup script (lists + archives them, confirming each repo name with you).
#
# HOST-SIDE ONLY. This value must never be added to a Compose service's
# environment. GITHUB_E2E_EXCHANGE_TOKEN below is a SEPARATE credential that DOES
# enter the api container — separate, so that this one does not have to.
GITHUB_E2E_PAT_TOKEN=

# --- GitHub create-new-repo test-exchange token (plan row 66) — TEST-ONLY ---
# A SECOND, SEPARATE GitHub credential — the only one that ever enters a product
# container. It exists so the browser create-new-repo round trip can be driven end
# to end WITHOUT putting GITHUB_E2E_PAT_TOKEN, the harness's own credential, into a
# container: separate values can be revoked independently, and the "the harness PAT
# enters no container" property above stays literally true.
#
# Why a second token instead of reusing the one above:
# the create-new-repo flow's second hop is `POST /login/oauth/access_token`, a
# SERVER-side call from the api. Driving it from a browser spec means the
# containerised api must be able to complete that exchange. Plan row 66 does this
# with a test-only exchange route, double-gated exactly like POST /v1/test/seed
# (NODE_ENV !== 'production' AND SUPAGLOO_ENABLE_TEST_SEED=1 — the route is not
# even registered otherwise). That route returns THIS token as the user access
# token.
#
# WHAT IT CAN ACTUALLY DO — stated accurately, because six documents once claimed a
# narrowness that is not obtainable:
#   * It needs to CREATE a repository (`POST /user/repos`), which is the whole point
#     of the create-new-repo flow.
#   * **Any GitHub token that can create repositories on an account can also DELETE
#     them.** Fine-grained `Administration: write` is the SAME permission
#     `DELETE /repos/{owner}/{repo}` requires, and `delete_repo` is a CLASSIC-PAT-ONLY
#     scope, so "deliberately no delete_repo" is a no-op phrase for a fine-grained
#     token. There is NO create-without-delete credential to mint. Do not go looking
#     for one, and do not describe this token as having one.
#   * A classic PAT with `repo` (the same shape as GITHUB_E2E_PAT_TOKEN above) is
#     what is deployed and is sufficient. Mint at:
#     https://github.com/settings/tokens/new
#
# THE ACTUAL MITIGATIONS, none of which is "the token is narrow":
#   1. The route is double-gated and is NOT REGISTERED unless both gates pass, so it
#      does not exist in a production image.
#   2. The variable is READ only when that route registers — a plain
#      `docker compose up` never sets SUPAGLOO_ENABLE_TEST_SEED, so the value is
#      never read at all.
#   3. The route verifies the POSTed `client_id`/`client_secret` against
#      GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID/GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET (timing-safe) before answering,
#      so reaching the api's published port is no longer sufficient to obtain it.
#   4. GITHUB_E2E_PAT_TOKEN still enters no container, and this is a DISTINCT value,
#      so revoking one does not require revoking the other.
#
# RESIDUAL RISK, recorded rather than argued away (design-delta §11.8/§11.9): while
# the test overlay is up, a broadly-scoped GitHub credential for an account that also
# holds your real repositories sits in the api container's environment, and the api
# publishes 4000:4000 on every interface. The mitigation that would genuinely shrink
# the blast radius is a DEDICATED THROWAWAY / BOT ACCOUNT (the exit design-delta §11.9
# already names), not a narrower scope — no narrower scope exists.
#
# The other half of that mechanism needs no secret and is therefore NOT a variable
# you set here: `docker-compose.test.yml` also sets
# GITHUB_OAUTH_INTERNAL_BASE_URL=http://api:4000 on the api service, so the
# SERVER-side token exchange goes to the api itself. The PUBLIC
# GITHUB_OAUTH_BASE_URL stays real github.com — it is the URL your BROWSER opens,
# and pointing it at a Docker hostname is the DNS_PROBE_FINISHED_NXDOMAIN failure
# plan row 62 item (e) was about.
#
# Reaches the api container by ${GITHUB_E2E_EXCHANGE_TOKEN} substitution from
# THIS file's untracked sibling `.env` — never inline the literal value in any
# tracked file, including docker-compose*.yml.
#
# Absent value: the test-only exchange route fails fast naming this variable. It
# never falls back to a placeholder, and it never silently disables itself — a
# spec that quietly stopped exercising the real exchange is a green lie.
GITHUB_E2E_EXCHANGE_TOKEN=

# OPTIONAL. Which account's App installation the e2e suites should use, matched
# case-insensitively against `account.login` from GET /app/installations.
#
# Leave it BLANK when the App has exactly one installation — the harness adopts
# that one, so the suite runs with zero extra config. Set it only when the App is
# installed for more than one account, in which case the harness REFUSES to guess
# (a wrong guess creates throwaway repos in someone else's account) and the error
# lists the logins it found.
#
# The installation ID itself is never configured: it is discovered at runtime
# (design-delta §11 / D5). A hardcoded id is exactly what broke plan row 62
# item (d) — the worker was always on real GitHub, 404ing on a fabricated id.
# Install the App at: https://github.com/apps/<GITHUB_APP_SLUG>/installations/new
SUPAGLOO_E2E_GITHUB_OWNER=

# --- S3 / MinIO (dual endpoint scheme — the presigned-URL gotcha) ---
# S3_ENDPOINT        : internal Docker-network address; server-side/container ops.
# S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT : host-reachable address; used to SIGN presigned URLs that a
#                      host-side browser/client must be able to reach.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_BUCKET=supagloo-dev
S3_ACCESS_KEY=supagloo
S3_SECRET_KEY=supagloo-dev
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# --- Provider stubs: NONE. All four providers are real in e2e. ---
# There are deliberately no *_STUB_URL variables here any more.
#
# Task #9 introduced five stub servers under `tests/stubs/**`; task 34-E8 deleted the
# openrouter/gloo/youversion ones; task 62 (design-delta §11, D20) deleted the last
# two — the GitHub REST stub and the local git smart-HTTP server — along with the
# whole directory. Every e2e lane in root/api/dbos/nextjs now reaches real
# github.com and api.github.com, using the GitHub App creds above plus
# GITHUB_E2E_PAT_TOKEN for throwaway fixture-repo lifecycle.
#
# UNIT suites keep their stubs and mocks: no unit lane makes network egress.
#
# Accepted, recorded cost: the git-ops e2e lanes no longer run offline.
#
# Reclaiming the throwaway repos an e2e sweep leaves behind (~15-20 private repos
# per full sweep, in an account that also holds your real repos) is a deliberate
# HUMAN action — there is no in-suite teardown and no CI-able fast path:
#
#     npm run cleanup:github-e2e             # prompts per repo, ARCHIVES, never deletes
#     npm run cleanup:github-e2e -- --dry-run # just list the candidates

# --- E2E provider secrets (design-delta §10.8, task 34-E2) ---
# The real-provider gating e2e suites (api/dbos, tasks 34-E3/34-E4/34-E8) hit the
# LIVE OpenRouter / Gloo / YouVersion APIs. The three vars below are REQUIRED: a
# missing one must FAIL the e2e global-setup fast with an actionable message — a
# gating suite that silently skips its provider tests is a green lie. Only
# YOUVERSION_E2E_ACCESS_TOKEN (further down) is deliberately optional. CI wiring is
# out of scope (design-delta §9-Q12); this is the local-dev `.env` story only.

# YouVersion Platform API key (already wired — dbos sends it as a header).
#
# ONE VALUE, TWO SPELLINGS (plan row 43 / D43.3). The backends read it as
# YOUVERSION_APP_KEY; supagloo-nextjs reads it as YV_APP_KEY. That divergence is
# pre-existing and is bridged at the Compose boundary — the `nextjs` service block in
# docker-compose.yml carries `YV_APP_KEY: ${YOUVERSION_APP_KEY}`, so setting it HERE is
# what makes the nextjs container boot. Before row 43 the nextjs service had no
# `environment:` block at all and the key reached neither the build nor the container.
# nextjs also fails closed on it: an empty YV_APP_KEY is a boot REFUSAL (a thrown
# `instrumentation.ts` register()), not a 500 page. And note the inverse rule stated in
# the SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY block above: nextjs must never receive that variable.
YOUVERSION_APP_KEY=

# Real, live-verifiable Gloo AI client credentials. api-e2e drives the real connect
# routes with these; dbos-e2e live-mints a Gloo token before writing an encrypted row.
GLOO_CLIENT_ID=
GLOO_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The APP-UNDER-TEST's Gloo credentials, deliberately DISTINCT names from the pair above
# (design-delta §10.8, task 34-E2). GLOO_CLIENT_ID/SECRET configure STAGEHAND's own LLM;
# these two are what supagloo-nextjs's browser lane connects INTO the product, so a single
# shared pair would let a spec pass while proving nothing about the product's own connect
# route. Read by `supagloo-nextjs/lib/gloo/harness-creds.ts`, whose e2e globalSetup loads
# THIS file. Documented here (previously only in supagloo-nextjs/.env.example) because this
# is the file a developer actually edits.
GLOO_CONNECT_CLIENT_ID=
GLOO_CONNECT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# A DEDICATED, LOW-BALANCE OpenRouter key (design-delta §10.3/§10.9) — the low cap is
# the backstop against a runaway suite. Never reuse a production key here.
OPENROUTER_E2E_TEST_API_KEY=

# Optional (the ONE deliberately-optional real spec, design-delta §10.4b): a dedicated
# test account's YouVersion access token for the live userinfo round-trip (34-E6).
# When unset, that spec skips LOUDLY (never silently). All the vars above do NOT skip.
YOUVERSION_E2E_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Task #36 renderWorkflow (dbos service; design-delta §7 workflow 9). ALL OPTIONAL —
# docker-compose.yml passes them through with the env schema's defaults. The three
# *_TIMEOUT_SECONDS are child-process KILL DEADLINES standing in for DBOS's non-existent
# per-step timeout. The two model ids are the FALLBACK used only when a manifest carries
# no cached narration/music asset ref; unset means the render proceeds WITHOUT that track
# rather than failing (model ids are never hardcoded, §10.9).
RENDER_MEDIA_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=
RENDER_BUNDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=
RENDER_INSTALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=
RENDER_NARRATION_MODEL=
RENDER_MUSIC_MODEL=

# How often `renderWorkflow` re-checks the RenderJob row for an operator cancel while a
# child process is running. Optional; the dbos env schema defaults it.
RENDER_CANCEL_POLL_SECONDS=

# Plan row 45 (§9-Q8) — Remotion's OWN `concurrency` for `renderMedia`, i.e. how many
# Chromium tabs encode frames in parallel INSIDE one render. Distinct from the `render`
# QUEUE's concurrency, which is firm at 1 render per worker and is not configurable.
#
# DELIBERATELY UNSET. Remotion defaults it to half the machine's CPU threads, and each
# unit is a Chromium tab holding decoded frames — the single largest memory lever in the
# pipeline, and the usual reason a container with a modest memory limit OOMs on a render
# that succeeds on a laptop. api and dbos are not deployed to Railway
# (current-design §6:932-935), so any recommended number is extrapolated rather than
# measured in place; see `docs/render-sizing.md` for the measured Compose numbers and the
# extrapolation. Set it when you constrain the container's memory, not before.
RENDER_MEDIA_CONCURRENCY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plan row 42 — the nightly `cleanupOrphanedAssetsWorkflow` (dbos service). Both OPTIONAL
# with real defaults, so the stack boots unchanged if you leave them blank.
#
# CLEANUP_RETENTION_HOURS (default 168 = 7 days): how long a failed/canceled job's S3
# objects survive before the sweep deletes them. It is also a SAFETY property — the
# Compose dbos container runs this schedule nightly against the SAME MinIO bucket and app
# database every e2e lane uses, and a 7-day window keeps freshly-seeded fixtures (age:
# seconds) far outside the delete set. Do not shorten it below a working day.
#
# CLEANUP_DRY_RUN (default false): when true the sweep computes and LOGS the exact delete
# set and performs no S3 or database mutation. Set it to `true` the first time you point
# this at an environment whose contents you care about.
CLEANUP_RETENTION_HOURS=
CLEANUP_DRY_RUN=
